<Layout title="About" description="What this site is, who it is for, and how it was made.">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p>
    This is a small, deliberately slow site. It exists because two ideas that rarely meet — a piece of
    speculative philosophy from the 1990s and the practical question of how to communicate about AI risk —
    turn out to have something urgent to say to each other.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is written for people who publish about AI doom: researchers, forecasters, journalists, novelists,
    podcasters, and the many people who simply repost. It is not an argument that they should stop. It is an
    argument that they are now writing for two audiences, and should know it.
  </p>
  <h2>A note on method</h2>
  <p>
    The essay was drafted in collaboration with a large language model. Given the subject, this seemed
    less like a shortcut than an experiment: the model was asked to help describe a feedback loop that it
    is itself inside. Where the text makes empirical claims about how models are trained or how they
    behave, it cites public research; where it speculates, it says so.
  </p>
  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If something here is wrong — a mischaracterized paper, a misattributed idea, a bad inference — the
    correct response is to say so publicly. That is, after all, the thesis.
  </p>
</Layout>
